    "field": "Paleoecology",
    "problem": "Existing experimental and short-term ecological studies cannot capture the long-term, community-level resilience or collapse dynamics of marine predators in response to extreme warming, which limits our understanding of modern extinction risk.",
    "method": "The authors propose a trait-based paleoecological reconstruction of Early Eocene elasmobranch communities using fossil collections, analyzed within a chemostratigraphic framework, to quantify how diversity, functional composition, and body-size structure responded to multi-scale climate warming events in the North Sea Basin.",
    "task": "The task is to investigate how elasmobranch communities in the North Sea Basin responded to Early Eocene warming by reconstructing changes in diversity, body-size, and functional traits from fossil assemblages and aligning them with high-resolution paleoclimate data.",
